<?php

/**
 * @file
 * Uninstall behaviour for the headless patients roster.
 *
 * ## There is no schema here any more
 *
 * This module keeps no tables, and the only one it ever had was a mistake.
 *
 * `headless_patients_intake_review` was created to hold intake review state,
 * because nothing in the codebase recorded it. That was wrong on both counts:
 * the site has recorded it for years as the `intake_processed_cs` flag on the
 * submission, written by `custom_module`'s `FlagIntakeForm()`, and a parallel
 * store for a fact that already has a home is only ever going to disagree with
 * it. It did, immediately and in the worst direction: the table started empty,
 * so all 188 of the clinic's submissions reported as `new` and the chiropractor
 * was shown work that had already been done.
 *
 * {@see \Drupal\headless_patients\PatientIntakeService} now reads and writes the
 * flag, so there is nothing here to create. {@see headless_patients_update_8003()}
 * drops the table on installations that have it.
 *
 * ## Why there is no permission-granting code here either
 *
 * This file used to declare the roles that may read and write the roster, grant
 * them `access headless patients` and `manage headless patients`, and revoke those
 * permissions from `patient_chirothin` and the two patient roles. All of that is
 * gone, along with the permissions themselves, for two reasons.
 *
 * The grants never took effect: the routing file put `_permission` under
 * `defaults`, where Drupal does not read it, so nothing on these routes was ever
 * gated. An operator reading this file would reasonably conclude the endpoints were
 * closed to patients when in fact every route was open to any signed-in account.
 *
 * And the arrangement itself was the problem. The role lists here answered "who is
 * a chiropractor" a third time, in a module about patients, and they had already
 * drifted from headless_progress, which grants its own permission to the patient
 * roles. Access is now expressed once, in the headless_access module, by
 * {@see \Drupal\headless_access\PortalRoles} and the policy classes that read it.
 * Roles are the single source of truth; there is nothing to grant.
 */

/**
 * Implements hook_update_N().
 *
 * Drops the review table, which is no longer read or written.
 *
 * ## Why 8003 and not 8002
 *
 * 8002 created this table on installations that predated it, and it never ran:
 * the module was reinstalled instead, and installing sets the recorded schema
 * version to the highest update number available at that moment. So the site
 * believes 8002 has already happened and will never call it. Reusing the number
 * would look like the fix but silently do nothing, which is precisely how the
 * table went missing in the first place.
 *
 * Safe to run against a site where the table does not exist, and safe against
 * rows that matter: the table was never the record of anything real.
 */
function headless_patients_update_8003(): void {
  headless_patients_drop_review_table();
}

/**
 * Drops the obsolete review table, if this site still has it.
 *
 * Shared by the update hook and hook_uninstall() because they cannot see each
 * other: uninstalling does not run update hooks, so a site that uninstalls before
 * it ever runs updatedb would otherwise leave the table behind with nothing left
 * in code that knows its name.
 */
function headless_patients_drop_review_table(): void {
  $database = \Drupal::database();
  $name = 'headless_patients_intake_review';

  if (!$database->schema()->tableExists($name)) {
    return;
  }

  $database->schema()->dropTable($name);

  \Drupal::logger('headless_patients')->notice(
    'Dropped the @table table. Intake review state is the @flag flag.',
    ['@table' => $name, '@flag' => 'intake_processed_cs']
  );
}

/**
 * Implements hook_uninstall().
 */
function headless_patients_uninstall(): void {
  headless_patients_drop_review_table();

  drupal_flush_all_caches();
}
